<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry I, Duke of Brabant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1165">1165</a>–<a href="/years/1235">1235</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry I of Brabant, Henry the Courageous</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry I, Duke of Brabant, was the first ruler of the Louvain dynasty to use the title Duke of Brabant, reigning from 1183 to 1235. He founded towns, granted charters and expanded his lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, though he was defeated by the Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège at Steppes in 1213. His reign turned the old County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a> into the Duchy of Brabant, one of the leading principalities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-i-duke-of-brabant.jpg" alt="Henry I, Duke of Brabant" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numisantica · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hertog_Brabant_Hendrik_I_denier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry, called the Courageous, was born about 1165, possibly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, the son of Count Godfrey III of Louvain and Margaret, daughter of Duke Henry II of Limburg. His father also held the titles of landgrave of Brabant, duke of Lower Lorraine and margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. Henry appeared early as co-ruler with his father. In 1180 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-i-countess-of-boulogne">Matilda of Boulogne</a>, daughter of Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> and Matthew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, and on that occasion received the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> from his father. He acted as regent while Godfrey went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1182 to 1184.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry took the title of duke of Brabant in 1183, and after his father's death in 1190 King Henry VI confirmed the elevation, while abolishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Duchy of Lower Lorraine</a> in practice by creating the empty title of duke of Lothier. Henry quarrelled with Count Baldwin V of Hainaut and, with Duke Henry III of Limburg and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> of Guelders, in further conflicts.</p><p>In mid-1197 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of Henry VI, took part in the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and on news of the death of Count Henry II of Champagne, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and acted as regent until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aimery-of-cyprus">Aimery of Cyprus</a> arrived. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he supported Otto IV against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, then changed sides in 1204. He fought alongside Otto IV at Bouvines in 1214 and was defeated. In 1213 he suffered a heavy defeat against the Bishopric of Liège at the Battle of Steppes, and he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> in 1217–1218.</p><p>Under Henry there was town policy and town planning. He used the creation of new towns as an instrument in the political organisation of regions, and gave city rights and trade privileges to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a> and Eindhoven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1235 Emperor Frederick II appointed Henry to bring his fiancée Isabella, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Henry fell ill on the way back and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> on 5 September 1235. He was buried in Saint Peter's Church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>. His tomb there is the oldest of its kind to survive, showing him as an idealised smiling young man in a long robe and the duke's cloak, holding a sceptre. It was damaged during the French occupation in the eighteenth century and restored in the mid-19th century. His bones were dug up in 1929 and returned to the tomb in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry I of Brabant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Steppes in 1213 the militia of Liège and its allies defeated Duke <b>Henry I of Brabant</b>, a victory long celebrated in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1210" aria-label="Leuven on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4657 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry I, Duke of Brabant"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5107 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5107 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1210">What was here in 1210? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
